1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,800
Hello everyone, this is Group 14,
Team Sweet Potatoes.

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:05,400
Our project focuses on how to enable

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:09,600
international residents to participate
in local government decisions.

4
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:13,900
During our investigation,
we found five problems.

5
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:18,900
First, residents find policy terminology
hard to understand,

6
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:22,800
and they lose confidence
when expressing their opinions.

7
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:28,900
Second, it's hard to attend
in-person discussions with a busy daily life.

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,600
Third, topics that don't feel
personally relevant get less attention.

9
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:39,600
Fourth, residents get stuck
finding the right department.

10
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,350
Lastly, newer residents
rely on social media,

11
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:50,200
but official information is mostly
distributed through physical channels.

12
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:52,000
Meet Jason.

13
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:55,900
He moved from Taiwan eight years ago.

14
00:00:56,010 --> 00:01:01,900
He works full time, and he has a dog.

15
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:07,700
Like most of the people we interviewed,
he doesn't follow council decisions closely.

16
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:12,200
But when something affects him,
he runs into the same problems.

17
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,950
So, we introduce HearHere:

18
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,250
a street pillar paired with a mobile app,

19
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:22,100
with a customised AI helper inside.

20
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:25,200
Let's see what Jason can do with HearHere.

21
00:01:25,390 --> 00:01:29,000
On his way to the park,
Jason passes a HearHere pillar.

22
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:32,850
He taps the screen and finds
the dog park issue nearby.

23
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:37,100
It's shown in Traditional Chinese,
with difficult terms explained.

24
00:01:37,390 --> 00:01:40,100
Then he speaks to the AI helper in Mandarin.

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,050
It turns his words into
a clear English comment

26
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,450
and names the responsible department.

27
00:01:45,710 --> 00:01:47,550
Jason checks it before sending.

28
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:49,900
His voice is never recorded.

29
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:54,750
He taps his phone on the NFC pad,
and the issue moves to the app.

30
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,550
The pillar keeps nothing.

31
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:00,600
In the app, he can follow
every stage through to the outcome.

32
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,000
He can also share it on Instagram,
so his neighbours can join in.

33
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,150
And if he has more questions,

34
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:19,600
he can ask Spud in the app,
in his own language.

35
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:27,850
With HearHere, Jason can understand
the policy in his own language,

36
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:31,900
find the right department,
and know his opinion has been received,

37
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,200
all without attending a meeting.

38
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,393
But HearHere has limits:

39
00:02:37,473 --> 00:02:40,313
speaking in public may feel uncomfortable,

40
00:02:40,393 --> 00:02:42,969
and AI translation can make mistakes.

41
00:02:43,049 --> 00:02:46,152
Next, we'll test comfort,
translation accuracy,

42
00:02:46,232 --> 00:02:49,650
access without smartphones,
and how councils respond.

43
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,891
HearHere: the street pillar
for understanding local decisions.

44
00:02:52,971 --> 00:02:54,350
Speak here. Be heard!
